% dossier-function: original two-part loop and arranging lab for Grade 7: a melody group and a bass group play an eight-bar minor loop, then the class decides how much drops out in bars 5-6 and how the bass returns, while listening for how Kabza De Small's production uses the bass as an arranging voice
% dossier-elements: eight bars in 4/4, F minor (four flats); chords Fm | Bbm | Db | Cm | Fm | Bbm | Db-Cm | Fm; melody (Keys) written range Db4-Db5, pitch set F G Ab Bb C Db Eb; bass written range G2-F3 in bass clef; melody rhythm z2 X3 Y Z2 (two beats' rest, a dotted quarter from beat 2, an eighth on the "and" of 3, a quarter) in bars 1, 2, 3 and 5, varied in bars 4, 6 and 7, half-note cadence on F4 in bar 8; bass pattern root quarter, eighth rest, root eighth on the "and" of 2, quarter rest, fifth below on beat 4 (bars 1-4), silent in bars 5-6, a split-bar pattern in bar 7 and a closing root in bar 8; largest melodic leap a perfect fourth
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 112 is the target; start at 88. F minor is the rehearsal key for concert band instruments (G minor for Bb instruments); the page's Transpose can move it to E minor or D minor for recorders and ukuleles. The melody's written range Db4-Db5 sits inside the young-unison C4-D5; the bass part is for bass bars, bass ukulele, cello, keyboard left hand or a low synth. Teach the bass rhythm first by clapping (one, and-of-two, four), then the melody's late entry on beat 2, then put them together; only then try the drop and the cues. Turn the page's Band bass down in the mixer while students play the written bass
% dossier-accompaniment: The written chords drive the page's lo-fi hip-hop style (electric piano, soft kit, held seventh chords), chosen for its spacious keyboard harmony; it is a stand-in and is not amapiano. No log-drum patch, shaker groove, piano riff or vocal chop from amapiano production is imitated; the students' bass part plays the arranging role the bridge asks them to listen for
% dossier-lyrics: Instrumental: keyboard, bells, recorder or voices on a neutral syllable for the melody; bass instruments for the bass. No words are supplied, and no words or vocal lines from "Sponono" are used
% dossier-honesty: Original AI-assisted course composition generated for Grand Central Stationery on 11 November 2026 under human-directed range, texture and teaching constraints. It is not traditional, not collected from a community, not modelled on the reference's melody and not an amapiano pastiche; the listening reference is a bridge only. Drafted as "Log-Drum Constellation"; retitled because its own respect note says the log drum must not be detached from amapiano, and the piece contains no log drum
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the melody, bass line, chord loop, improvisation cues and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. "Sponono" and its recording remain their owners' copyright and are not included
% dossier-respect: Name Kabza De Small (Kabelo Motha) and the featured artists, say that amapiano grew in Gauteng's townships (Pretoria and Johannesburg) in the 2010s from kwaito, deep house and jazz, carried by producers, DJs and dancers, and that the log drum is a signature sound that belongs to that scene. Do not detach the log drum from amapiano and market it as a generic preset, and never present this classroom loop as amapiano
% dossier-review: South African amapiano producer or educator, Grade 7 music teacher, second score reader and student listening panel; all reviews still required
