N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit of the Keys melody: bar 1 (Fm) two beats' rest, C5 dotted quarter, Ab4 eighth, F4 quarter; bar 2 (Bbm) rest, Bb4, F4, Db4 in the same rhythm; bar 3 (Db) rest, F4, Ab4, Db5, the arpeggio turned upward; bar 4 (Cm) C5 quarter, Bb4-G4 eighths, Eb4 and G4 quarters; bar 5 (Fm) as bar 1; bar 6 (Bbm) rest, Bb4 quarter, C5-Db5 eighths, Bb4 quarter; bar 7 (Db then Cm) eighth rest, F4-Ab4-Db5 eighths, C5 quarter, Bb4-G4 eighths; bar 8 (Fm) Ab4 and G4 quarters, F4 half. Bass: bars 1-4 root quarter, eighth rest, root eighth, quarter rest, the chord's fifth below as a quarter on beat 4 (F3/C3, Bb2/F3, Db3/Ab2, C3/G2); bars 5-6 whole-bar rests (the drop); bar 7 Db3 quarter, rest, Db3 eighth, C3 quarter, rest, G2 eighth; bar 8 F3 quarter, rest, F3 eighth, F3 quarter, quarter rest. Every bar of both parts totals eight eighths; melody range Db4-Db5, largest leap a perfect fourth; bass range G2-F3. The batch draft was a single line of rising arpeggios with an Eb5-Eb4 octave drop into bar 8 and no bass-register part despite claiming one. Originality check: the melody and bass are not the "Sponono" hook, its log-drum line or its piano chords; arpeggiated minor-triad melodies and root-fifth bass patterns are common property.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether the late entries on beat 2 are steady for Grade 7 players at quarter note = 112, whether the lo-fi style's swing feel suits the straight bass part, and whether students hear the drop as an arranging choice have not been tested. An amapiano producer or educator must judge whether the framing and the new title keep the piece clear of imitation; a classroom trial, a second score reader and a student listening panel remain required before publication.
